{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-212682",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}$ such that $|x|\\ne |y|.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in B$ to see which elements $y\\in B$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=-1,$  then the value $y=0$ satisfies $|x|\\ne |y|.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(-1,0).$\n\n- If $x=0,$ then the values $y=-1$ and $y=1$ satisfy $|x|\\ne |y|.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(0,-1)$ and $(0,1).$\n\n- If $x=1,$  then the value $y=0$ satisfies $|x|\\ne |y|.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,0).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(-1,0),(0,-1),(0,1),(1,0)}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
